Application Autoscaling
Objective
•	Automatically scale the number of pods in a deployment based on load metrics like CPU or memory.
•	Ensure high availability, performance, and efficient resource usage without manual intervention.
________________________________________
2. Key Concepts
Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)
•	Monitors metrics (CPU/memory) and adjusts the number of pods in a deployment, replica set, or stateful set.
•	Works as a control loop running inside the Kubernetes controller manager.
•	Default check interval: every 15 seconds.
Autoscaling Process
1.	HPA retrieves the target metric from its definition.
2.	Collects current metric values for each pod from the metrics server.
3.	Computes usage percentage for each pod based on resource requests.
4.	Averages usage across all targeted pods.
5.	Determines scaling ratio and adjusts pod count (up/down) accordingly.
Why HPA?
•	High Availability: Adds pods during spikes.
•	Cost Efficiency: Removes pods when load is low.
•	Operational Efficiency: Reduces human intervention.
________________________________________
3. Prerequisites
•	Pods must have resource requests (CPU/memory) defined.
Without requests, HPA cannot calculate utilization.
________________________________________
4. Creating an HPA
a. Using oc autoscale Command
oc autoscale deployment/hello --min 1 --max 10 --cpu-percent 80
•	Maintains 1–10 pods, targeting 80% average CPU utilization.
b. Using a YAML Manifest
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: hello
spec:
  minReplicas: 1
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        averageUtilization: 80
    type: Resource
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: hello
•	minReplicas / maxReplicas → pod limits
•	averageUtilization → target CPU or memory usage
•	scaleTargetRef → resource to scale (deployment, etc.)
Memory-based scaling:
metrics:
- resource:
    name: memory
    target:
      averageUtilization: 80
Note: Memory-based scaling is not ideal for JVM apps or apps that pre-allocate memory.
c. Using Web Console
•	Developer perspective → Topology → Select deployment → Actions → Add Horizontal Pod Autoscaler.
•	Set min/max pods and metric target (CPU or memory).
________________________________________
5. Monitoring HPA
View HPA Status
oc get hpa
# TARGETS may show <unknown> initially until metrics are collected
Describe HPA for Events
oc describe hpa hello
•	Shows scaling events, target utilization, min/max pods, and last scaling actions.
View Pod Metrics
oc adm top pods -n <project>
________________________________________
6. Notes
•	HPA scales only pods with defined resource requests.
•	CPU limit throttles pod execution; memory limit kills pods if exceeded (OOMKilled).
•	Min/Max replicas accommodate bursts and prevent cluster overload.
•	HPA is automatic; you do not manually adjust replicas during scaling events.

